Forensic Acquisition Report – Phase 2  
Windows 11 Virtual Machine Forensic Data Collection Using Kali Linux

Overview

In this phase, I moved from planning into hands-on forensic work. The goal was to collect real forensic evidence from a controlled Windows 11 virtual machine using Kali Linux as the investigation tool.

This phase focused on setting up a target system, acquiring evidence (disk, memory, and network data), and properly documenting everything so it could be analyzed in later phases.


Target System Setup

Platform
Local virtual machine (VMware Workstation)

Operating System
Windows 11 Pro

Purpose of Target
The Windows 11 VM was configured as a simulated user workstation. A basic “incident” was created by executing a suspicious file (`update.exe`) to simulate malware behavior and generate forensic artifacts.


Acquisition Plan

The plan was to collect three main types of forensic evidence:

- Disk image of the Windows 11 system  
- Memory dump from the active running system  
- Network traffic during simulated execution  

Tools Selected (from Phase 1)
- dc3dd → disk imaging  
- WinPMEM → memory acquisition  
- tcpdump → network capture  
- Autopsy / Volatility → later analysis tools  

These tools were chosen because they cover the three core areas of digital forensics: disk, memory, and network activity.


